From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Sep 2026 06:10:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] diagnostics: a real stack walk at a C++ throw, and who raised
 a message box.

Two read-only diagnostics, plus the correction of a third that did not work.

**ntdll: the call chain at a C++ throw (+cxxthrow).** An MSVC throw carries
its ThrowInfo in ExceptionInformation, which names the *type*
(scripts/pe-throwtype.py) but never the throw site, because every C++
exception in the process is raised from the same two instructions. This walks
the stack at dispatch_exception, where the context is still the thrower's,
using RtlLookupFunctionEntry + RtlVirtualUnwind, and falling back to "pop the
return address" for leaf functions and for thunks with no unwind info (Office's
CFG dispatch stubs are three bytes and have none). Its own channel, because
Office raises and catches C++ exceptions as ordinary control flow.

Two things about where and how it is done are the point, both learned by
getting them wrong first:

  - **Not in RaiseException.** On x86_64 that is hand-written assembly
    (`__ASM_GLOBAL_FUNC`, "some DRMs depend on RaiseException not altering
    non-volatile registers"); a hook added to the portable C version beside it
    compiles into a build that never runs it, and the resulting silence looks
    exactly like "nothing threw". Disassembling the built dll is what caught
    it. The +seh addresses that seemed to contradict the hook being called at
    all (throws at kernelbase +0xD907, RaiseException entry at +0xD898) were
    never a contradiction: +0xD907 is the return address of
    `call RtlRaiseException`, because +seh prints context->Rip.

  - **Not a stack scan.** A scan cannot tell a live frame from a stale one.
    Against PowerPoint's failing start-up the scan version reported
    oart.dll +0x44ae0 -- the *success* path of a buffer allocator, sitting on
    the stack from an earlier call -- as if it were on the chain. The unwind
    version produced the real chain, from the throw helper through oart and
    ppcore to POWERPNT.EXE's main, and that chain is what located the
    GetProcAddress("MathBuildUp") that fails.

**user32: the caller of a MessageBox (+msgbox).** Every MessageBox funnels
through MessageBoxIndirectW, so it is the one place that can answer "who put
that box up?" -- a question Office makes expensive because its dialogs name
symptoms ("not enough memory or system resources") and not the code path that
gave up. Note the delay-load thunks above the real caller: the "USER32.dll"
and "MessageBoxW" strings that appear are that mechanism, not data.

**kernelbase: a stack scan at an unhandled fault.** Kept as a scan, because
the faults it is for land in a three-byte CFG thunk with no unwind info *and*
arrive after the crashing context is already partly gone; winedbg produces no
backtrace for them at all. It found the caller that identified the missing
IWebProviderError::Properties. Read its first entries and distrust the rest.

All three are TRACE/MESSAGE only and change no behaviour.
--- a/dlls/ntdll/exception.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/exception.c
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
 #include "ntdll_misc.h"
 
 WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(seh);
+WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(cxxthrow);
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(threadname);
 
 typedef struct
@@ -190,11 +191,81 @@
 /*******************************************************************
  *		dispatch_exception
  */
+/* Diagnostic, off unless WINEDEBUG has +cxxthrow.
+ *
+ * An MSVC C++ throw arrives here with its ThrowInfo in ExceptionInformation[2]
+ * and the throwing module's base in [3], which names the *type* (feed the
+ * difference to scripts/pe-throwtype.py). What it never gives is where the
+ * throw came from: every C++ exception in the process is raised from the same
+ * couple of instructions inside RaiseException.
+ *
+ * This is the right place for that, and RaiseException is not: on x86_64 that
+ * function is hand-written assembly (`__ASM_GLOBAL_FUNC`, because "some DRMs
+ * depend on RaiseException not altering non-volatile registers"), so a C hook
+ * added to the portable version next to it compiles into a build that never
+ * runs it -- which is exactly the mistake this replaces. Here the stack is
+ * still the thrower's, untouched by any unwinding.
+ *
+ * Its own channel because Office raises and catches C++ exceptions as ordinary
+ * control flow, dozens per second; this is for one question at a time.
+ */
+static void trace_cxx_throw_stack( const EXCEPTION_RECORD *rec, const CONTEXT *context )
+{
+#ifdef __x86_64__
+    CONTEXT ctx = *context;
+    int frame;
+
+    if (rec->ExceptionCode != 0xe06d7363 || rec->NumberParameters < 4) return;
+    if (!TRACE_ON(cxxthrow)) return;
+
+    TRACE_(cxxthrow)( "C++ throw: ThrowInfo %p, module base %p, rva %#Ix\n",
+                      (void *)rec->ExceptionInformation[2],
+                      (void *)rec->ExceptionInformation[3],
+                      rec->ExceptionInformation[2] > rec->ExceptionInformation[3] ?
+                          rec->ExceptionInformation[2] - rec->ExceptionInformation[3] : 0 );
+
+    /* A real unwind, not a stack scan. The scan this replaces could not tell a
+     * live frame from a stale one -- it reported an allocator's *success* path
+     * as if it were on the call chain -- and the distinction is the whole
+     * question here. At the moment an exception is dispatched the context is
+     * still the thrower's and every module involved carries .pdata, so
+     * RtlVirtualUnwind gives the actual chain. */
+    for (frame = 0; frame < 24 && ctx.Rip; frame++)
+    {
+        RUNTIME_FUNCTION *func;
+        LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY *mod = NULL;
+        ULONG64 base = 0, frame_base = 0;
+        void *handler_data;
+
+        if (LdrFindEntryForAddress( (void *)ctx.Rip, &mod ))
+            TRACE_(cxxthrow)( "  #%-2d %p\n", frame, (void *)ctx.Rip );
+        else
+            TRACE_(cxxthrow)( "  #%-2d %p  %s +%#Ix\n", frame, (void *)ctx.Rip,
+                              debugstr_w(mod->BaseDllName.Buffer),
+                              (ULONG_PTR)ctx.Rip - (ULONG_PTR)mod->DllBase );
+
+        if (!(func = RtlLookupFunctionEntry( ctx.Rip, &base, NULL )))
+        {
+            /* A leaf function, or a thunk with no unwind info (Office's CFG
+             * dispatch stubs are three bytes and have none). The x64 ABI says
+             * such a frame pushed nothing but its return address. */
+            ctx.Rip = *(ULONG64 *)ctx.Rsp;
+            ctx.Rsp += sizeof(ULONG64);
+            continue;
+        }
+        RtlVirtualUnwind( UNW_FLAG_NHANDLER, base, ctx.Rip, func,
+                          &ctx, &handler_data, &frame_base, NULL );
+    }
+#endif
+}
+
 NTSTATUS WINAPI dispatch_exception( EXCEPTION_RECORD *rec, CONTEXT *context )
 {
     NTSTATUS status;
     DWORD i;
 
+    trace_cxx_throw_stack( rec, context );
+
     switch (rec->ExceptionCode)
     {
     case EXCEPTION_WINE_STUB:
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
@@ -41,6 +41,7 @@
 
 WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(seh);
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(winedbg);
+WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(cxxthrow);
 
 typedef INT (WINAPI *MessageBoxA_funcptr)(HWND,LPCSTR,LPCSTR,UINT);
 typedef INT (WINAPI *MessageBoxW_funcptr)(HWND,LPCWSTR,LPCWSTR,UINT);
@@ -391,6 +392,24 @@
 {
     EXCEPTION_RECORD record;
 
+    /* Diagnostic, off unless WINEDEBUG has +cxxthrow. An MSVC C++ throw comes
+     * through here, and its ExceptionInformation carries the ThrowInfo (args[2])
+     * and the throwing module's base (args[3]) -- enough to name the *type*
+     * with scripts/pe-throwtype.py, but not to say where it was thrown from,
+     * because this function is the throw site for every C++ exception in the
+     * process. Scanning the stack here supplies the missing half.
+     *
+     * Deliberately its own channel: Office raises and catches C++ exceptions
+     * as ordinary control flow, dozens per second, so this is unusable as part
+     * of +seh and is meant to be turned on for one question at a time. */
+    if (code == 0xe06d7363 && count >= 4 && args && TRACE_ON(cxxthrow))
+    {
+        MESSAGE( "wine: C++ throw, ThrowInfo %p base %p (rva %#Ix):\n",
+                 (void *)args[2], (void *)args[3],
+                 args[2] > args[3] ? args[2] - args[3] : 0 );
+        stack_scan_from( (const ULONG_PTR *)&record, "the throw" );
+    }
+
     record.ExceptionCode    = code;
     record.ExceptionFlags   = flags & EXCEPTION_NONCONTINUABLE;
     record.ExceptionRecord  = NULL;
@@ -527,6 +546,63 @@
 
 
 /******************************************************************
+ *		dump_stack_scan
+ *
+ * Diagnostic only. Print the qwords near RSP that point into a loaded
+ * module's code, with the module they belong to and their offset in it.
+ *
+ * Written because winedbg's own backtrace does not appear for these faults
+ * (it starts, prints two dbghelp fixmes, and stops), and because the faults
+ * this is aimed at land inside a *CFG dispatch thunk* -- three bytes,
+ * `jmp rax` -- which has no useful unwind info, so a proper RtlVirtualUnwind
+ * from there cannot walk out either. A raw scan needs neither: the `call`
+ * that entered the thunk pushed its return address and it is still sitting
+ * at the top of the stack, and the frames above it are still there too.
+ *
+ * It is a scan, so it prints stale addresses as well as live ones. Read it
+ * as "these functions are somewhere in this thread's recent history", not as
+ * an exact call chain -- the first one or two entries are the ones that
+ * matter, and they are the ones a thunk-shaped fault otherwise hides.
+ */
+static void stack_scan_from( const ULONG_PTR *sp, const char *what )
+{
+#ifdef __x86_64__
+    const NT_TIB *tib = (const NT_TIB *)NtCurrentTeb();
+    const ULONG_PTR *limit;
+    int shown = 0, i;
+
+    limit = (const ULONG_PTR *)tib->StackBase;
+    if (sp < (const ULONG_PTR *)tib->StackLimit || sp >= limit) return;
+
+    MESSAGE( "wine: stack scan for %s from %p:\n", what, sp );
+    for (i = 0; sp + i < limit && i < 256 && shown < 20; i++)
+    {
+        ULONG_PTR v = sp[i];
+        void *base = NULL;
+        WCHAR path[MAX_PATH];
+        const WCHAR *name;
+
+        if (v < 0x10000) continue;
+        if (!RtlPcToFileHeader( (void *)v, &base ) || !base) continue;
+        if (!GetModuleFileNameW( base, path, ARRAY_SIZE(path) )) continue;
+        name = wcsrchr( path, '\\' );
+        name = name ? name + 1 : path;
+        MESSAGE( "wine:   %p  %s +0x%Ix\n", (void *)v,
+                 debugstr_w(name), (ULONG_PTR)v - (ULONG_PTR)base );
+        shown++;
+    }
+#endif
+}
+
+static void dump_stack_scan( const EXCEPTION_POINTERS *ptr )
+{
+#ifdef __x86_64__
+    if (!ptr->ContextRecord) return;
+    stack_scan_from( (const ULONG_PTR *)ptr->ContextRecord->Rsp, "the fault" );
+#endif
+}
+
+/******************************************************************
  *		start_debugger
  *
  * Does the effective debugger startup according to 'format'
@@ -545,6 +621,7 @@
 
     format_exception_msg( epointers, buffer, sizeof(buffer) );
     MESSAGE( "wine: %s (thread %04lx), starting debugger...\n", buffer, GetCurrentThreadId() );
+    dump_stack_scan( epointers );
 
     InitializeObjectAttributes( &attr, &nameW, 0, 0, NULL );
     if (!NtOpenKey( &dbg_key, KEY_READ, &attr ))
--- a/dlls/user32/msgbox.c
+++ b/dlls/user32/msgbox.c
@@ -458,6 +458,52 @@
 /**************************************************************************
  *      MessageBoxTimeoutW (USER32.@)
  */
+/* Diagnostic. Every MessageBox in this tree funnels through here, so this is
+ * the one place that can answer "who put that box on the screen?" -- a
+ * question Office makes expensive to answer any other way, because its
+ * dialogs say things like "not enough memory or system resources" that name a
+ * symptom and not the code path that gave up.
+ *
+ * Same technique as the one in kernelbase's start_debugger: scan the stack for
+ * qwords that point into a loaded module and print them module-relative. It is
+ * a scan, so it shows stale frames too; the first few entries are the ones
+ * that matter. Off unless WINEDEBUG has +msgbox.
+ */
+/* Declared in winnt.h behind a guard this file does not trip. */
+PVOID WINAPI RtlPcToFileHeader(PVOID,PVOID*);
+
+static void trace_msgbox_caller( LPCWSTR text )
+{
+#ifdef __x86_64__
+    const NT_TIB *tib = (const NT_TIB *)NtCurrentTeb();
+    const ULONG_PTR *sp, *limit;
+    int shown = 0, i;
+
+    if (!TRACE_ON(msgbox)) return;
+    sp = (const ULONG_PTR *)&text;
+    limit = (const ULONG_PTR *)tib->StackBase;
+    if (sp < (const ULONG_PTR *)tib->StackLimit || sp >= limit) return;
+
+    TRACE_(msgbox)( "message box %s raised by:\n", debugstr_w(text) );
+    for (i = 0; sp + i < limit && i < 400 && shown < 16; i++)
+    {
+        ULONG_PTR v = sp[i];
+        void *base = NULL;
+        WCHAR path[MAX_PATH];
+        const WCHAR *name;
+
+        if (v < 0x10000) continue;
+        if (!RtlPcToFileHeader( (void *)v, &base ) || !base) continue;
+        if (!GetModuleFileNameW( base, path, ARRAY_SIZE(path) )) continue;
+        name = wcsrchr( path, '\\' );
+        name = name ? name + 1 : path;
+        TRACE_(msgbox)( "  %p  %s +0x%Ix\n", (void *)v, debugstr_w(name),
+                        (ULONG_PTR)v - (ULONG_PTR)base );
+        shown++;
+    }
+#endif
+}
+
 INT WINAPI MessageBoxTimeoutW( HWND hWnd, LPCWSTR text, LPCWSTR title,
                                UINT type, WORD langid, DWORD timeout )
 {
@@ -523,6 +569,8 @@
     UINT i;
     struct ThreadWindows threadWindows;
 
+    trace_msgbox_caller( msgbox->lpszText );
+
     if (!(hRes = FindResourceExW(user32_module, (LPWSTR)RT_DIALOG, L"MSGBOX", msgbox->dwLanguageId)))
     {
         if (!msgbox->dwLanguageId ||
